WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.300
Hannah Montana's best friend, a secretary who's&nbsp;
not impressed by ghosts, and Mr. Incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.560
himself. If the cast of "Young Sheldon"&nbsp;
looks familiar, there's a good reason why.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.360
The star of "Young Sheldon," Iain Armitage&nbsp;
was only nine years old when the prequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.260
series premiered in September&nbsp;
of 2017. But believe it or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.640
this wasn’t the first time he appeared in&nbsp;
front of the camera. Years before the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:28.200
Armitage made a name for himself on YouTube,&nbsp;
running his channel "Iain Loves Theatre,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.340
where he hosted a series of reviews of&nbsp;
stage plays and musicals like "Hamilton."

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:40.920
"One of the most tremendous, amazing,&nbsp;
fantastic - Better than fantastic shows ever!"

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.080
Gaining some level of notoriety thanks to his&nbsp;
YouTube series, Armitage made his unintentional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.300
TV debut in an episode of "Impractical Jokers,"&nbsp;
before getting official casting calls. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.160
first came in an episode of "Law &amp; Order: Special&nbsp;
Victims Unit," ahead of snagging roles in a string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.160
of 2017 indie films. This includes "The Last&nbsp;
Castle," "Our Souls at Night" and "I'm Not Here"

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.520
While starring on "Young Sheldon,"&nbsp;
Armitage has had a recurring role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.160
as Ziggy Chapman on "Big Little Lies" and has even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.820
done voiceover work for cartoons like&nbsp;
"Scoob!" and "Paw Patrol: The Movie."

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.060
Playing Sheldon's father George Cooper Sr. is&nbsp;
actor Lance Barber. Oddly enough, he actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.740
a previous appearance on "The Big Bang Theory"&nbsp;
as a peer of Sheldon's in a Season 5 episode. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.120
must have impressed producers, because several&nbsp;
years later he re-appeared as Sheldon's dad.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.220
Barber's very first TV role came in an episode&nbsp;
of "ER," and he quickly made a career as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.960
guest starring actor across a number of big name&nbsp;
productions. This includes appearances in episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.840
of "Gilmore Girls," "Californication," and the&nbsp;
detective comedy "Monk." In 2005, Barber joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.820
a show's main cast for the first time, playing&nbsp;
Paulie in the Lisa Kudrow sitcom "The Comeback."

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.480
If you recognize Barber from anywhere&nbsp;
though, it's probably his recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.079
role as Bill Ponderosa in "It's&nbsp;
Always Sunny in Philadelphia."

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:57.300
"How is drinking a glass of milk&nbsp;
going to loosen me up Bill?"

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.360
"I-I spiked the milk with&nbsp;
methylenedioxypyrovalerone."

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:00.860
"What?"

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.297
"It's bath salts."

00:02:02.297 --> 00:02:06.480
Every family sitcom needs a great mom,&nbsp;
and "Young Sheldon" has Zoe Perry as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.860
Mary Cooper. Perry can actually trace her&nbsp;
TV career all the way back to the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.820
when she played Jackie Harris in a pair&nbsp;
of episodes of "Roseanne" that revisited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.840
the youth of its title character. This is&nbsp;
doubly ironic, because the older version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.700
of Jackie was played in that series by Laurie&nbsp;
Metcalf, who also plays the older version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.740
Mary Cooper on "The Big Bang Theory." Oh, did we&nbsp;
mention that Metcalf is Perry’s actual mother?

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.420
Perry would have to wait more than a decade&nbsp;
before her next TV appearance, which came in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.340
2006 episode of "Law &amp; Order: Criminal Intent."&nbsp;
She seemed to find a niche in legal dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.180
though, popping up for single-story&nbsp;
parts on "Cold Case," "Deception," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.320
"Conviction" over the next few years, before&nbsp;
landing a recurring role on "The Family."

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.760
Just before starring on "Young Sheldon,"&nbsp;
Perry had closed out a nine-episode stint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.520
as political spoiler Samantha Ruland on "Scandal."

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.800
To play the role of Grandma Cooper — better&nbsp;
known as Meemaw — "Young Sheldon" enlisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.260
the services of '80s icon Annie Potts, who fans&nbsp;
will probably recognize from her role in the 1984&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.480
classic "Ghostbusters," where she played cynical&nbsp;
receptionist Janine Melnitz. She'd come back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.340
reprise the role in 2021 for the legacy sequel,&nbsp;
"Ghostbusters: Afterlife," too. In between,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.780
of course, Potts had a long and successful&nbsp;
career, both on TV and at the movies.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.100
In cinemas, you might know Potts from movies&nbsp;
like "Pretty in Pink" or the cult classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:28.440
Whoopi Goldberg comedy "Jumpin' Jack Flash."&nbsp;
On TV, Potts starred for four seasons on "Any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:34.560
Day Now" between 1998 and 2002, before landing&nbsp;
a recurring role on "Joan of Arcadia" in 2004.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.720
Other recurring TV parts saw Potts appearing&nbsp;
in four episodes of "Law &amp; Order: SVU" and five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.060
episodes of "Chicago Med," while she also had a&nbsp;
role on the short-lived Leslie Bibb comedy "GCB."

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.320
In addition to doing work for cartoons&nbsp;
like the 2020 reboot of "The Rocketeer"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.820
and "Johnny Bravo," Potts might be best known as&nbsp;
the voice of Bo Peep in the "Toy Story" franchise.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:58.380
"What do you say I get someone&nbsp;
else to watch the sheep tonight?"

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.220
"Oh Yeah, right."

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:06.660
While he's appeared in every episode of "Young&nbsp;
Sheldon," as well as a single flashback episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:11.700
of "The Big Bang Theory," Montana Jordan's&nbsp;
part as George Jr. is actually one of just two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.660
on-screen roles in his career, so it's not likely&nbsp;
that you'd recognize him from anywhere else.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.740
His other role, though, comes in the film&nbsp;
"The Legacy of a Whitetail Deer Hunter,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.960
a 2018 Netflix movie from writer-director&nbsp;
Jody Hill. The film stars Josh Brolin as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.580
a hunter named Buck Ferguson who brings his&nbsp;
friend and cameraman Don on a weekend sojourn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.780
into the woods with his son Jaden. Montana,&nbsp;
of course, plays Jaden, and while the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.760
didn't garner many positive reviews, its cast&nbsp;
is often cited as one of its few bright spots.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.640
Unlike her co-star and on-screen brother Montana&nbsp;
Jordan, Raegan Revord's resume stretches back a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.660
bit further beyond "Young Sheldon," beginning&nbsp;
in 2014, when she was just six years old.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.800
That year, she made her television debut in&nbsp;
an episode of “Modern Family,” and would go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.660
on to reprise that role a season later. In 2015,&nbsp;
she appeared in an episode of the Netflix series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.940
"W/ Bob and David," a revival of sorts of Bob&nbsp;
Odenkirk and David Cross' '90s cable comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:07.260
"Mr. Show with Bob and David." From there,&nbsp;
Revord showed up alongside Lily Tomlin and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:12.600
Jane Fonda on Netflix's "Grace and Frankie," as&nbsp;
well as on the similarly-titled "Alexa &amp; Katie."

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.560
Annie Potts isn't the only "Toy Story" alum&nbsp;
to have a major role on "Young Sheldon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.820
The other is Wallace Shawn, who plays&nbsp;
Meemaw's boyfriend, Dr. Sturgis. Shawn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.860
a veteran character actor with an unmistakable&nbsp;
nasally voice, gives life to the ever-frightened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.240
Rex in the "Toy Story" films. If you're a child&nbsp;
of the '80s, though, you probably know Shawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.560
best for his role as the villainous Vizzini, in&nbsp;
the Rob Reiner classic, "The Princess Bride."

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.270
"He didn't fall?! Inconceivable!"

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.840
"You keep using that word - I do not&nbsp;
think it means what you think it means."

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.680
Children of the '90s, however, remember&nbsp;
Shawn as the lovelorn Mr. Hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:50.400
in "Clueless." He returned to the role&nbsp;
again for the "Clueless" sitcom spin-off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.240
which aired for three seasons. Sci-fi&nbsp;
fans will remember Shawn best for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.660
recurring role on "Star Trek: Deep Space&nbsp;
Nine," where he played the cantankerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:02.100
Grand Nagus Zek. Teens in the 2000s might&nbsp;
know Shawn from his stint on "Gossip Girl."

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.260
Other memorable roles in his career include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:07.680
runs on "Crossing Jordan," "The&nbsp;
Cosby Show," and "Murphy Brown."

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.080
Of all of the cast of the series, Ed Begley&nbsp;
Jr. might have one of the longest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.180
prolific filmographies, which begins all the way&nbsp;
back in the 1960s, with his acting debut in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:23.040
episode of "My Three Sons." Over the decades,&nbsp;
he's accumulated more than 300 credited roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.760
but we can try to give you a brief overview&nbsp;
of some of his most important work.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.480
After shooting to stardom with his&nbsp;
leading role on the '80s medical drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.980
"St. Elsewhere," Begley Jr. spent the next&nbsp;
few decades appearing in various hit shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:39.000
and movies, including "The Love Boat,"&nbsp;
"Roseanne," and "3rd Rock From the Sun."

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.280
In the 2000s, Begley Jr. continued playing&nbsp;
doctor roles, on "7th Heaven" as Dr. Hastings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.600
and on "Kingdom Hospital" as Dr. Jesse&nbsp;
James. He also had the memorable role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.800
of Stan Sitwell on "Arrested Development."&nbsp;
Though we can't possibly rattle off every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.340
part he's ever played, we couldn't let&nbsp;
his bio be complete without mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:59.580
his role as Hiram Gunderson on the&nbsp;
critically acclaimed "Six Feet Under."

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:05.100
Pastor Jeff often serves as the foil for "Young&nbsp;
Sheldon," engaging the scientifically-minded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.240
Sheldon in some surprisingly hard-hitting&nbsp;
discussions on science versus religion.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:14.280
Playing the quasi-villain on the show is actor&nbsp;
Matt Hobby, who appeared on HBO's acclaimed period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.320
crime drama "Boardwalk Empire," with a recurring&nbsp;
role in Season 3 as Phillip. A few years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.760
he took a part in The CW drama "Hart of&nbsp;
Dixie," and not long before he debuted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:27.180
on "Young Sheldon," Hobby held a role&nbsp;
on the Rob Lowe comedy "The Grinder."

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:32.760
To most kids, the phys ed teacher was either&nbsp;
the nicest member of the faculty or the meanest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.500
and fortunately for Sheldon, Wayne Wilkins is&nbsp;
the former, the friendly and affable school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.660
coach. In fact, Wilkins takes center stage in&nbsp;
his own story, dealing with the breakdown of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:47.940
his marriage while leaning on his friend George&nbsp;
Cooper Sr. for support. He's played by Doc Farrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.960
a journeyman actor who counts Wilkins as&nbsp;
his first ever recurring role on television.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.460
Prior to the series, Farrow had only ever&nbsp;
appeared in single episodes of other shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.300
starting out playing mostly unnamed&nbsp;
background characters. Since appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.380
on "Young Sheldon," he's also popped up in&nbsp;
small roles on "Curb Your Enthusiasm" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.100
"Arrested Development," as well as in an&nbsp;
episode of the revival of "Will &amp; Grace."

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.320
On "Young Sheldon," Melisa Peterman plays the&nbsp;
role of nosy neighbor Brenda Sparks, the mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.820
of Billy and Bobby who works at the local bowling&nbsp;
alley. She's played by Melissa Peterman, who made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.500
her acting debut back in 1996 as a background&nbsp;
character in the Coen Brothers classic "Fargo."

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:27.180
Despite her cinematic beginnings, Peterman's&nbsp;
career has been almost entirely on television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.260
beginning with an episode of "Just Shoot&nbsp;
Me" in 2000. If you recognize her, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:35.460
it's probably from her starring role in the&nbsp;
long-running family sitcom "Reba" with Reba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:40.260
McIntire, where she played the religious Barbra&nbsp;
Jean Hart. If you watch country music game shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.200
though, it's almost certain that you&nbsp;
know her as the host of "The Singing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.280
Bee," a competition that tossed together&nbsp;
elements of karaoke and spelling bee.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.600
From there, Peterman became a fill-in host&nbsp;
or celebrity guest on the game show circuit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.900
so you might recognize her from&nbsp;
episodes of "Home &amp; Family" and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.200
$100,000 Pyramid," or "Dancing&nbsp;
Fools," just to name a few.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:02.820
"Young Sheldon" has recruited its fair share&nbsp;
of '80s stars, but there's still one more:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:07.020
Craig T. Nelson, who plays Dale Ballard.&nbsp;
Owner of the local sporting goods store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.100
Ballard is also Missy Cooper's baseball&nbsp;
coach — an appropriate role, considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:15.540
Nelson is easily best known for playing the title&nbsp;
character on the '90s classic sitcom "Coach."

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.860
Prior to "Coach," Nelson had recurring roles&nbsp;
on "Private Benjamin" and was part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:24.780
main cast of the crime drama "Chicago Story" in&nbsp;
the '80s. Beyond that stretch of success on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.980
though, Nelson has also had plenty of hits&nbsp;
at the movies. He starred in classics like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.300
"Poltergeist," "Action Jackson," and "Turner&nbsp;
and Hooch" in the '80s. Following his run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.780
on "Coach," Nelson appeared in "The&nbsp;
Devil's Advocate" with Keanu Reeves.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:40.980
But while many audiences may recognize&nbsp;
Nelson's face from one of his many famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:46.320
films or popular TV shows, it's also his voice&nbsp;
that might stand out to you. That's because in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.760
"The Incredibles" and its sequel, Nelson&nbsp;
played Bob Parr, Mr. Incredible himself.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:55.380
"Young Sheldon" has seen its share of&nbsp;
surprising breakout recurring guest stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:59.520
and audiences were introduced to another&nbsp;
in Season 5 with the arrival of Mandy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.780
the older woman who George Jr. becomes&nbsp;
smitten with. She's played by Emily Osment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.560
sister of former child actor and&nbsp;
"Sixth Sense" star Haley Joel Osment.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:12.420
Starting her career the same year that "The Sixth&nbsp;
Sense" premiered, Osment's first acting job was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.500
the film "The Secret Life of Girls," though it's&nbsp;
more likely you'd recognize her from her role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:21.960
Gerti Giggles in the second and third "Spy Kids"&nbsp;
movies. But her biggest role by far began in 2006,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:27.480
when she starred alongside Miley Cyrus on Disney's&nbsp;
iconic series "Hanna Montana" as best friend Lilly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.220
Truscott. From there, Osment moved to more serious&nbsp;
fare, starring in the Crackle thriller "Cleaners"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:37.320
in 2013, while also doing voice work for cartoons&nbsp;
like "Kick Buttkowski and "Rainbow Brite."

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:41.040
More recently, Osment has had starring&nbsp;
roles on a pair of short-lived dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:46.020
"Almost Family" and "Pretty Smart," as well as a&nbsp;
recurring part on Michael Douglas' "The Kominsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:50.400
Method." If you were a kid in the late 2000s,&nbsp;
though, you might also know Osment for her music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.660
as she released an album in 2010, and shot&nbsp;
a number of music videos to accompany it.

